<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch08_P01_Root_four</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The proof that \(\sqrt{2}\) is irrational can be copied almost word for word for \(\sqrt{4}\), as below. But \(\sqrt{4} = 2 = \dfrac{2}{1}\) is rational, so this ‘proof’ must go wrong somewhere.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Assume that \(\sqrt{4}\) is rational.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(\sqrt{4} = \dfrac{p}{q}\) for some integers \(p\) and \(q\) with \(q \neq 0\), and we may choose the fraction \(\dfrac{p}{q}\) in its simplest form.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Squaring, \(4 = \dfrac{p^2}{q^2}\), so \(p^2 = 4q^2\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>So \(p^2\) is even, and therefore \(p\) is even.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>So \(p = 2k\) for some integer \(k\), and \(p^2 = 4k^2\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>From lines 3 and 5, \(4q^2 = 4k^2\), so \(q^2 = k^2\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>So \(q^2\) is even, and therefore \(q\) is even.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>So \(p\) and \(q\) are both even, and \(\dfrac{p}{q}\) is not in its simplest form. This contradicts line 2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>So the assumption in line 1 is false, and \(\sqrt{4}\) is irrational.</td></tr>
</table>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Which is the first line that does not follow from the lines before it?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Give an example of integers \(p\) and \(q\), with \(q \neq 0\) and \(\dfrac{p}{q}\) in its simplest form, that satisfy line 3.</p>
<div>\(p =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] \(q =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Test the lines with actual numbers. Take \(p = 2\) and \(q = 1\): \(\sqrt{4} = \dfrac{2}{1}\), in its simplest form. Line 3: \(p^2 = 4 = 4 \times 1^2 = 4q^2\). Line 4: \(p^2 = 4\) is even, and \(p = 2\) is even. Line 5: \(k = 1\). Line 6: \(q^2 = 1 = k^2\). Line 7 claims that \(q^2\) is even, but \(q^2 = 1\).</p>
<p>So line 7 is the first line that does not follow: from \(q^2 = k^2\) nothing follows about whether \(q^2\) is even. In the proof for \(\sqrt{2}\) the same step gave \(q^2 = 2k^2\), which is even. Lines 8 and 9 follow from line 7, so once line 7 fails they prove nothing.</p>
<p>The examples for part (b) are \(p = 2, q = 1\), and the same numbers with signs changed. If \(p^2 = 4q^2\) and \(\dfrac{p}{q}\) is in its simplest form, then \(q = \pm 1\), so \(q\) is always odd.</p>
<p>The method of a proof is not a recipe that works for every number: each step has to be checked again when the proof is copied to a new case.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
